      "content": "That’s perfect. The \"simplified numeric abstraction\" line is exactly the kind of high-level dismissal we can use to make the auditors feel like they're just looking at the surface level while we're looking at the real science.\n\nI think we have the core of the narrative. One last thing to tighten up before I finalize the deck: I need to be prepared for the inevitable \"fairness\" argument. Someone is going to say, \"Regardless of how deep the encounters are, if one side wins 85% of the time, the game isn't fun for the 15% who keep losing; it's just a slaughter.\"\n\nCan you give me a final, definitive way to recontextualize that \"slaughter\" or \"unbalanced\" feeling? I want to frame the 15% loss rate not as 'failure' or 'unbalance,' but as a necessary component of the 'player tension' or 'competitive scarcity' that makes the Xylos experience so high-stakes. I need a punchy, technical way to justify why a massive win-rate disparity is actually essential for the \"Intense Engagement\" model we're pitching.",
